{%- comment -%}
  ci-store-footer.liquid — global storefront footer (SPA).
  The exact company line is locked by brand standards / Magic Build Prompt v3:
  "Crema Italia, LLC · Lutz, Florida, USA · cremaitalia.com".
{%- endcomment -%}
<footer class="ci-footer" role="contentinfo">
  {%- comment -%}
    Email capture, added 2026-08-18. The POC storefront had NONE - no newsletter field,
    no signup, nothing - while templates/password.liquid and live-theme/templates/index.liquid
    (the coming-soon pages this storefront replaces) both have one. Launching as-was would have
    retired the only acquisition surface on the same day paid traffic begins, and the win-back /
    abandoned-cart / 60-day-grace campaigns in the Store Operating Standards all assume a list
    that nothing was building.

    Deliberately quiet: one sentence, one field, no modal, no popup, no discount bribe - a
    discount incentive would also contradict the "we issue no codes, ever" rule (Standard v1.3).

    PROD: replace with Shopify's native {% raw %}{% form 'customer' %}{% endraw %} newsletter form
    (tags the customer for the chosen email platform), or the ESP's own embed once one is chosen.
    Email platform is still an open launch-gating decision (CLAUDE.md §10). This send is MOCKED.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="footer-signup">
    <p>We write to inform... a note when a roaster or coffee is added, or if one is about to run out. Never spam. We grind out only freshly roasted updates.</p>
    <form class="fs-row" id="footer-signup-form" onsubmit="return submitFooterSignup(event)" novalidate>
      <label for="fs-email" class="visually-hidden">Email address</label>
      <input id="fs-email" name="email" type="email" placeholder="Your email" autocomplete="email" required>
      <button type="submit">Keep me posted</button>
    </form>
    <p class="fs-note" id="fs-note">No codes, no daily mail. Unsubscribe in one click.</p>
  </div>
  {%- comment -%}
    Policy links, added POC22 (2026-08-22). Four Shopify-hosted policies went live 2026-08-22 and
    nothing on the storefront linked them; Shopify links them from the CHECKOUT footer automatically,
    but a customer deciding whether to trust the store never reaches checkout.

    Two decisions worth not re-litigating (docs/legal/README.md carries the full reasoning):

    1. FOUR NAMED LINKS, not one "Legal notices" link. There is no destination for a single link -
       /policies, /policies/ and /pages/policies all 404, so Shopify has no policy index route and a
       single link would need a page we build and maintain. The label would also collide with the
       Shopify "Legal notice" policy slot we deliberately left unset (it is the EU Impressum field,
       not required for US-only DTC).

    2. SHIPPING AND RETURNS SIT WITH THE USEFUL LINKS, not with the fine print. They are purchase
       content: a shopper wants the free-shipping threshold and the replacement promise BEFORE
       buying, and a "Legal" label signals fine print nobody clicks. Terms and Privacy genuinely are
       fine print and sit quietly beside the company line.

    Shipping points at the POLICY, not at a POC page. #page-shipping was removed in this batch - it
    was a 417-character condensed paraphrase of the published shipping policy (same threshold, same
    $8.50, same carriers, same transit bands), i.e. a second home for facts that would drift the
    moment either was edited. These links leave the SPA, which is the production-correct behaviour:
    in the real theme they are ordinary pages.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="footer-id">
    <span>Crema Italia, LLC &middot; Lutz, Florida, USA &middot; cremaitalia.com</span>
    <span class="footer-legal">
      <a href="/policies/terms-of-service">Terms</a>
      <a href="/policies/privacy-policy">Privacy</a>
    </span>
  </div>
  <div class="footer-links">
    <button onclick="showPage('promise')">The Crema Italia Promise</button>
    <a href="/policies/shipping-policy">Shipping</a>
    <a href="/policies/refund-policy">Returns</a>
    <button onclick="showPage('about')">About</button>
    <button onclick="showPage('faq')">FAQ</button>
    <button onclick="showPage('journal')">Journal</button>
    <button onclick="showPage('contact')">Contact</button>
  </div>
</footer>
